<h2>A Factory Half a Year Late</h2>

<p>A 40 GWh NMC 811 plant delayed by six months -- between lost market window, customer penalties, and equipment depreciation -- adds up to approximately $250 million. This estimate comes from McKinsey's Operations Practice analysis published in July 2026.</p>

<p>The problem is not isolated. The same report notes that gigafactories under construction worldwide are "pushing SOP back by six to nine months on average." Meanwhile, the report forecasts high-performance battery demand rising from approximately 1.6 TWh today to over 6.8 TWh by 2035 -- a gap that is widening.</p>

<p>Northvolt is a telling example. In March 2025, the European battery company filed for bankruptcy protection in Sweden. BMW cancelled a previously signed supply contract. According to media reports including Sifted, the core challenge was not technological, but about build capability and production ramp-up.</p>

<p>When an entire industry sees 6-9 month average delays, it is no longer a project management issue.</p>

<h2>Five Cracks in the Foundation</h2>

<p>McKinsey's report attributes gigafactory delivery delays to five root causes. Together, they reveal a structural problem: five dimensions of supply chain resilience fracturing simultaneously.</p>

<h3>One: Technical Specifications Still Changing During Construction</h3>

<p>A defining feature of gigafactories is concurrent development of product, process, and facility. Approvals are granted and construction begins while battery architecture and production processes are still being refined. Equipment is ordered to Specification A, only to arrive on-site and find the process has migrated to Specification B.</p>

<p>In supply chain terms, this is a "visibility" failure. In consumer goods, the logic is simple: define the SKU first, then purchase. A gigafactory breaks ground with an uncertain bill of materials.</p>

<h3>Two: Building with No Track Record</h3>

<p>The market demands the latest processes, but the latest processes have no historical production data. Suppliers say they can do it, but they haven't run that system at scale. Their pricing, lead times, and yield promises are estimates.</p>

<p>Buying standard equipment means buying certainty validated by thousands of production cycles. Buying first-of-its-kind equipment means buying an engineering process -- you and your supplier run the first lap together. When there is no learning curve, traditional forecasting models fail.</p>

<h3>Three: Equipment Concentrated in Asia</h3>

<p>Core process equipment for gigafactories comes primarily from two countries: China and South Korea. Whether building in Europe or North America, equipment ships from Asia -- weeks by sea, days for customs clearance and unpacking. When equipment fails, the supplier's technical team faces visa processing and long-distance travel.</p>

<p>McKinsey's report describes this challenge: supply chain concentration in Asia creates logistical complexity, challenges with transporting precision equipment over long distances, and difficulties accessing OEM support during installation and commissioning.</p>

<h3>Four: Scarcity of Experienced Delivery Leaders</h3>

<p>Dozens of gigafactories are operating or under construction worldwide, but managers with end-to-end delivery experience -- from site acquisition through equipment procurement, installation, commissioning, and ramp-up -- are insufficient. A decade ago the industry was making consumer electronics batteries. A decade from now, global battery demand is expected to quadruple. The pool of people who have "built the entire chain" cannot keep pace.</p>

<h3>Five: No Unified Project Orchestration</h3>

<p>A gigafactory involves dozens of stakeholders -- owner, general contractor, cleanroom contractor, equipment suppliers, technology licensors, local government, utilities, logistics providers -- each with independent schedules and contract terms. You assume the role of commander-in-chief without the authority.</p>

<h2>A Resilience Framework Lens</h2>

<p>These five root causes can be mapped onto Chowdhury and Quaddus's (2017) multidimensional supply chain resilience framework, grounded in dynamic capability theory, which decomposes resilience into proactive capability, reactive capability, and supply chain design quality.</p>

<p>The mapping: links one and two reflect proactive capability deficits (inadequate anticipation and buffer design before construction); links three and four reflect reactive capability deficits (lack of rapid response mechanisms); link five reflects supply chain design quality shortcomings (fundamental governance structure problems).</p>

<p>This reveals that the gigafactory challenge is not a local issue in any single link, but a system-wide weakness spanning anticipation, response, and governance.</p>

<h2>From Redundancy to Intelligence</h2>

<p>Traditional supply chain resilience follows a "redundancy for safety" path -- more inventory, more suppliers, more buffers. When Amazon's 2013 Christmas logistics collapsed, the response was more warehouses and more planes. The cost: capital efficiency.</p>

<p>McKinsey's report outlines an alternative: compressing coordination costs, accelerating information flow, and enabling dynamic resource reallocation to achieve stronger resilience with less physical buffer.</p>

<p>Specific approaches:</p>

<p>For uncertain specifications (link one), digital twins simulate the downstream impact of design changes in real time. The report notes that best-in-class companies use generative scheduling tools to automatically build resource-loaded plans.</p>

<p>For Asia-dependent supply chains (link three), embedding IoT sensors and visual AI in supplier production lines can detect deviations months before equipment reaches its destination.</p>

<p>For governance failures (link five), unified planning platforms keep all stakeholders on a single data source. Best-in-class practices include decision escalation fast tracks (4-hour response) and cross-enterprise integrated planning platforms.</p>

<p>For talent gaps (link four), the core solution is not hiring ready-made people but building knowledge management systems for replicable experience, establishing apprenticeship models, and giving project managers real resource authority.</p>

<h2>A Structural Challenge for Chinese Companies Going Global</h2>

<p>Chinese and Korean process equipment are the de facto global standard for battery manufacturing. But cross-border operations stretch lead times significantly and reduce fault response speed. This is not a procurement failure -- it reflects a structural tension between "reliable" (requiring concentrated, mature supply) and "controllable" (requiring distributed, fast-response capability).</p>

<p>This tension is most acute for Chinese battery companies building overseas. CATL in Hungary, Gotion in Germany, Envision in the UK and France -- all face the same challenges: equipment shipped from China, limited cross-border technical support, local talent shortages, and multi-country regulatory differences.</p>

<p>McKinsey's five-root-cause analysis applies here as well: these are not Asia-specific or Europe-specific problems, but universal challenges inherent to the gigafactory organizational form.</p>

<h2>The Industry's Real Divide</h2>

<p>Best-in-class operations achieve production in 18-24 months; the industry average takes longer. This gap is not closed by scale or money -- it is a gap in governance capability and digital maturity.</p>

<p>Three observations to close:</p>

<p>First, gigafactory delivery should not be a temporary project management function. It needs a permanent, global delivery capability center.</p>

<p>Second, don't layer digital tools on old processes. Redesign workflows first -- which tasks for AI, which for humans, how they collaborate -- then select technology.</p>

<p>Third, the "chain builder" talent gap should be managed as a strategic risk. The talent doesn't exist in the market; organizations must develop it themselves.</p>

